  (n = samples: the cleared book's own, or what the door adds. Cohort is the PREVIOUS cutoff's stocks, backstopped at the index's own 5y CAGR. `excess`/`bar` are the UNCAPPED book; (#330) the `ship` verdict decides and is read at top10 (excess/worst), the published basket — excess >= that column's `bar`, the p95 of 200 same-n refused cohorts entered at the cohort's own ranks, AND worst >= cleared -1.0, at 20y, 12y and 8y. `inert` = the door's names never reach the published basket)
  all entries (SIZED)          book +5.8%/yr  excess -0.8  win 32%  worst -8.7  OOS -0.3/-1.2   (windows 41, deployed 100%) [diagnostic: the book `size` funds, top-25; remainder earns the index]

── CORR-CAP probe: greedy top-10 by growth_score, skip names correlating >= cap with the kept book (36mo trailing), held 12y ──
  cap  off  book +5.7%/yr  excess -0.9  win 34%  worst -10.1  OOS -0.4/-1.4  [cap off = plain top-10 book]
  cap  0.8  book +5.8%/yr  excess -0.9  win 34%  worst -10.1  OOS -0.3/-1.4
  cap  0.6  book +5.8%/yr  excess -0.8  win 34%  worst -7.2  OOS -0.1/-1.5
  cap  0.4  book +6.2%/yr  excess -0.5  win 41%  worst -6.8  OOS +0.1/-1.0
  (a cap lifting book or worst with OOS both + -> ship as a book-construction rule; expectation low — every structure tweak since round 14 diluted.)

Caveats:
  • Peer-relative (#1): returns are de-meaned per ~6mo cutoff, so rho is SELECTION vs same-period
    peers (regime beta removed). A near-empty bucket has a weak peer set -> its rows count for less.
  • In-sample: knobs were hand-tuned on today's data; even the OOS split shares the regime.
  • Survivorship (#5): the universe is names that SURVIVED to today — dead tickers never enter,
    so realized returns are biased UP. Treat the edge as optimistic. Re-run with `stress` to inject losers.
  • Acquisition truncation (#112, UNMEASURED): the most common way a 20-year single-stock hold
    actually ENDS is neither bankruptcy nor a sell decision — it is being ACQUIRED. Compounding
    stops at a one-time premium, and the deal CRYSTALLISES the gain: it pays, on a date the holder
    never chose, the tax the deferral edge above assumes stays deferred. Nothing in this walk models
    a takeout, so every multi-year hold in the tables is one that was ALLOWED to run — optimistic in
    the same direction as the line above, by an amount this data cannot size. It argues mildly toward
    LARGER names (harder to swallow), the reasoning `growth_min_aum_etf` already accepts for funds.
  • Point-in-time (PIT ON): every cutoff was scored against the S&P 500 AS IT STOOD THAT DAY, so a
    name contributes no sample before it joined or after it left. This is the direct correction to
    the line above, and the edge is EXPECTED to fall — a lower number here is the honest one.
    15 pool name(s) were index members Yahoo no longer serves: fetched nothing, scored nothing,
    counted here rather than silently dropped. A big count means the correction is still incomplete.
    COVERAGE (#121): 45 of 61 index members in this pool produced a scoreable cutoff (74%).
    READ THE TWO NUMBERS TOGETHER: the miss count above is what Yahoo refused to answer, this is what the
    walk could actually score, and they are far apart because a dead name usually comes back as a payload
    with ZERO BARS — which counts as SERVED. A 0 above with a low ratio here does not mean the PIT
    correction is complete; it means the hole is not a fetch failure.
    TWO DRIVERS, AND THIS LINE CANNOT SPLIT THEM. (1) MECHANICAL: a 12y hold needs a full 12y
    forward window, so members that joined late are unscoreable at this horizon and NOT evidence of bias —
    which is why the ratio rises as the horizon shortens. (2) SURVIVORSHIP: measured 2026-08-22 over the
    734 members whose span carries an exit date — 387 served with ZERO BARS, 193 more with <36 in-span
    bars, only 154 scoreable — and ZERO of all 1206 members with a series ending over a year ago. Only
    (2) is bias, and it is the part that never shrinks with a shorter hold. So a name that DIED
    contributes nothing here rather than a loss, and Bessembinder's -91.95% median delisted return is
    absent from every number above. Closing it needs delisting DATES plus a terminal return, not a price
    backfill: these prices provably do not exist to fetch.
    COVERAGE FLOOR 1996-01-02: the source opens every pre-existing member's span on that date, so
    cutoffs before it are DROPPED rather than corrected — it bites the 20y run's front, not the 8y one.
    DEAD (#751): 278 of 1444 cutoffs paid a TERMINAL return, the one the line above asks for: a dead
    member's last close rolled into the S&P to the window's end, x0.7 if it lost over half its
    price in its last year (one constant: the archive records no delist reason). Archive names carry no
    fund rows (no SEC CIK map), so their growth fund term reads neutral; ^GSPC bars are quarterly.
  • Price-only (#6): no as-of P/E reconstructed, so the `value` multiplier is ×1.0 here; as-of DIVIDENDS are live since #53 and every dividend row below is real.
  • Overlapping 6-mo windows share price paths -> samples aren't independent; rho is directional.
  • Long-horizon (MAX monthly): only names alive for the FULL 12y window enter, so
    survivorship bias is WORSE than the daily path, and vol/MA are monthly-bar approximations.
backtest: 215 tickers, WALK-FORWARD scoring every ~6mo with a 12y forward holdout each (MAX monthly history)…
